1
00:00:05,597 --> 00:00:09,133
She wasn't someone
completely unrelated to your life.

2
00:00:09,997 --> 00:00:12,829
You took her to meet your parents.

3
00:00:12,829 --> 00:00:16,653
You're leaving now? Where are you going?

4
00:00:17,493 --> 00:00:19,933
To Mrs. Sonoda's husband's place?

5
00:00:23,385 --> 00:00:25,019
So what if I am?

6
00:00:25,019 --> 00:00:26,913
Can you say "Don't go"?

7
00:00:44,145 --> 00:00:48,333
[Episode 8]

8
00:00:59,781 --> 00:01:01,357
Oh, no...

9
00:01:01,357 --> 00:01:04,181
Oh, was I mistaken?

10
00:01:04,181 --> 00:01:06,273
I thought you were getting in.

11
00:01:09,069 --> 00:01:10,841
I'm getting in.

12
00:01:30,713 --> 00:01:32,529
These are really good.

13
00:01:32,529 --> 00:01:35,689
Disappearing is fun, isn't it? Want one?

14
00:01:35,689 --> 00:01:37,053
- Thanks.
- Go ahead.

15
00:01:37,053 --> 00:01:39,157
[June 21, 2025]

16
00:01:39,157 --> 00:01:41,445
Here.

17
00:01:41,445 --> 00:01:44,149
By the way, is it okay that
I sat right next to you?

18
00:01:44,149 --> 00:01:47,101
Sitting far apart on purpose would look
even more like we have something to hide.

19
00:01:47,101 --> 00:01:49,989
So no matter what we do now,

20
00:01:49,989 --> 00:01:52,829
some people would still scream
that this is an emotional affair.

21
00:01:52,829 --> 00:01:54,641
What's with this emotional affair stuff?

22
00:01:54,641 --> 00:01:58,761
I don't know. I just figured that
someone would say that.

23
00:01:58,761 --> 00:02:00,893
What's the good local food in Nagano again?

24
00:02:00,893 --> 00:02:02,833
And what should I get for a souvenir?

25
00:02:06,837 --> 00:02:08,800
Are you nervous?

26
00:02:09,549 --> 00:02:11,709
It's been about twenty years, so...

27
00:02:11,709 --> 00:02:13,537
Why now?

28
00:02:13,537 --> 00:02:16,164
My dad got sick,
and they say he doesn't have long,

29
00:02:16,164 --> 00:02:17,261
so I figured I should go now.

30
00:02:17,261 --> 00:02:20,901
- So it's like, "Let's go watch him die"?
- Come on, I'm not that twisted.

31
00:02:20,901 --> 00:02:23,193
Okay.

32
00:02:23,193 --> 00:02:26,749
But then again, maybe I am.

33
00:02:26,749 --> 00:02:29,949
If you're gonna see a parent this late,
this would be the only reason.

34
00:02:29,949 --> 00:02:31,933
Why else would you want to see him now?

35
00:02:31,933 --> 00:02:35,289
Just curious. I'm an adult now myself,

36
00:02:35,289 --> 00:02:38,281
so I wonder how I'll see him now.

37
00:02:38,281 --> 00:02:42,365
So you're running away from the wife
who eased your disappearing habit,

38
00:02:42,365 --> 00:02:46,337
and now you're going to
see the people who started it all?

39
00:02:48,053 --> 00:02:50,317
It's a pain, having parents.

40
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:53,933
If they're gone, the option's off the table,
so it makes things easier.

41
00:02:55,501 --> 00:02:58,166
After I go, I'll head right back to Sacchan.

42
00:03:00,045 --> 00:03:03,001
I don't really have anything to say.
Even though I'm going,

43
00:03:03,001 --> 00:03:06,181
I just plan to check on him
and then leave right away.

44
00:03:06,181 --> 00:03:08,869
And that'll be it
for your mini-disappearance?

45
00:03:08,869 --> 00:03:10,733
That'll be it.

46
00:03:14,817 --> 00:03:16,222
Well, this is the first time, so it's fine.

47
00:03:16,222 --> 00:03:18,093
Besides, I want to be home by dinner anyway.

48
00:03:18,093 --> 00:03:20,161
What do you want for lunch?

49
00:03:20,161 --> 00:03:22,861
We should be hitting a rest stop
in less than an hour.

50
00:03:22,861 --> 00:03:26,733
I love rest stop food.

51
00:03:26,733 --> 00:03:28,605
Can't wait.

52
00:03:47,709 --> 00:03:50,000
Mm, there's pumpkin in it.

53
00:03:51,533 --> 00:03:52,981
Yeah, you're right. It's good.

54
00:03:52,981 --> 00:03:55,101
- This is the best one.
- Yeah, I agree.

55
00:03:55,101 --> 00:03:56,141
Wait, you've had this before?

56
00:03:56,141 --> 00:04:00,389
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

57
00:04:02,321 --> 00:04:04,365
Mmm, delicious.

58
00:04:04,365 --> 00:04:07,933
Whoa, there's pumpkin in it.

59
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:09,737
No way.

60
00:04:10,881 --> 00:04:14,033
Whoa, you're right! This is tasty.

61
00:04:17,389 --> 00:04:21,300
Hang on, hang on. Let me get you some water.

62
00:04:32,357 --> 00:04:33,365
Are you okay?

63
00:04:33,365 --> 00:04:34,885
Sacchan, can you eat two?

64
00:04:34,885 --> 00:04:36,349
- Yeah, easy.
- Okay, then you can have this.

65
00:04:36,349 --> 00:04:38,389
Okay.

66
00:04:38,389 --> 00:04:41,393
Want me to go grab something else?

67
00:04:41,393 --> 00:04:43,613
I'm fine, it won't take much longer.

68
00:04:43,613 --> 00:04:45,433
Are you sure?

69
00:04:46,445 --> 00:04:50,433
You know, when you put an address
in the GPS and you're driving...

70
00:04:50,433 --> 00:04:51,433
Yeah.

71
00:04:51,433 --> 00:04:55,857
don't you just want to ignore it
and drive away from where you're going?

72
00:04:56,701 --> 00:04:59,633
Huh? Why?

73
00:05:00,569 --> 00:05:04,381
- Nah, I didn't really mean that.
- Hey, no lying! That's a fine.

74
00:05:04,381 --> 00:05:08,157
Hey, we never set up a fine system.

75
00:05:09,273 --> 00:05:13,925
But I get it. You do seem like that, Mitsuru.

76
00:05:14,649 --> 00:05:15,857
Like what?

77
00:05:15,857 --> 00:05:20,749
Like if someone blew on you,
you'd just float away somewhere.

78
00:05:20,749 --> 00:05:23,845
I thought that right when we first met.

79
00:05:23,845 --> 00:05:26,433
Maybe because you were surrounded by smoke.

80
00:05:27,217 --> 00:05:28,865
Like...

81
00:05:29,592 --> 00:05:33,357
I couldn't get a real grasp on you.

82
00:05:33,357 --> 00:05:38,193
But you turned out to be
a much more solid person than I thought.

83
00:05:40,029 --> 00:05:42,673
Maybe that's because
you're my anchor, Sacchan.

84
00:05:42,673 --> 00:05:44,741
You make sure I don't float away.

85
00:05:44,741 --> 00:05:48,265
If so, then I've got to
eat a lot more and get heavier.

86
00:05:55,409 --> 00:05:57,221
- Welcome.
- Hello.

87
00:05:57,221 --> 00:05:58,345
[Smiling Curry Buns]
Two curry buns, please.

88
00:05:58,345 --> 00:05:59,825
- That'll be 900 yen.
- Okay.

89
00:05:59,825 --> 00:06:01,933
- How are you paying?
- With this.

90
00:06:11,685 --> 00:06:14,285
Oh, crap. Is the bus leaving already?

91
00:06:14,285 --> 00:06:15,517
Just say something like,
"I think he missed the bus."

92
00:06:15,517 --> 00:06:18,193
Or something like,
"I fell asleep and didn't notice."

93
00:06:18,193 --> 00:06:19,233
I'll leave my phone too.

94
00:06:19,233 --> 00:06:21,785
There's nothing on it
that shouldn't be there.

95
00:06:21,785 --> 00:06:24,789
I probably won't be able
to make it next month,

96
00:06:24,789 --> 00:06:28,433
but I'll swing by the laundromat
on a third Friday sometime.

97
00:06:31,285 --> 00:06:35,497
Starting a disappearance from here
really is going hard mode, isn't it?

98
00:06:35,497 --> 00:06:38,221
Not really. There are plenty of cars.

99
00:06:38,221 --> 00:06:40,100
If you say so.

100
00:06:41,589 --> 00:06:46,317
So is this disappearing habit of yours
just a fundamental problem?

101
00:06:46,317 --> 00:06:48,413
How do you fix it?

102
00:06:48,413 --> 00:06:52,257
Sacchan was my anchor,
ensuring that I wouldn't run away.

103
00:06:52,257 --> 00:06:53,529
An anchor.

104
00:06:53,529 --> 00:06:58,361
But I realized
that's what's been pushing me to the edge.

105
00:06:59,221 --> 00:07:01,473
I kind of get it, but I also kind of don't.

106
00:07:04,165 --> 00:07:05,825
What about going to your parents' place?

107
00:07:05,825 --> 00:07:09,933
I'll pass. I remembered
a talk that I had with Sacchan.

108
00:07:11,081 --> 00:07:13,133
Here.

109
00:07:13,133 --> 00:07:15,233
Oh, a hearse.

110
00:07:16,573 --> 00:07:18,825
Look.

111
00:07:18,825 --> 00:07:20,941
- I'm being so inappropriate.
- You really are.

112
00:07:20,941 --> 00:07:23,309
Though I get not wanting to
see someone on their deathbed.

113
00:07:23,309 --> 00:07:25,100
I'm sorry.

114
00:07:26,385 --> 00:07:28,853
- You know, Sacchan,
- Yeah?

115
00:07:28,853 --> 00:07:32,537
you don't get along with
your parents very well, right?

116
00:07:32,537 --> 00:07:34,821
No, not very well at all.

117
00:07:34,821 --> 00:07:37,477
People like us,

118
00:07:37,477 --> 00:07:40,881
how will we feel when our parents die?

119
00:07:40,881 --> 00:07:46,129
We can't truly grieve,
so we'll just have to run away.

120
00:07:47,045 --> 00:07:50,405
- Run away?
- Yeah. Just not face it.

121
00:07:51,073 --> 00:07:53,033
Not like running away from our parents,

122
00:07:53,033 --> 00:07:56,966
but from ourselves
who can't even mourn our parents.

123
00:07:58,521 --> 00:08:02,100
Because it's wrong, isn't it? Not being sad.

124
00:08:02,941 --> 00:08:05,201
But I can't force it.

125
00:08:07,237 --> 00:08:10,213
I think I might get along with her.
I'd like to meet her someday.

126
00:08:10,213 --> 00:08:11,385
Do you like pumpkin?

127
00:08:11,385 --> 00:08:12,797
Wow, you're ignoring me.

128
00:08:12,797 --> 00:08:15,217
Pumpkin? Yes, I do.

129
00:08:15,217 --> 00:08:18,381
These curry buns have pumpkin in them
and are supposed to be good.

130
00:08:18,381 --> 00:08:20,373
I think you can probably handle two,
so please take them.

131
00:08:20,373 --> 00:08:22,765
Oh, thank you.

132
00:08:22,765 --> 00:08:24,409
See you.

133
00:08:24,409 --> 00:08:26,405
If you run away...

134
00:08:27,801 --> 00:08:32,609
Sacchan might end up hating you,
or she might end up miserable.

135
00:08:32,609 --> 00:08:34,401
Are you really okay with that?

136
00:08:36,873 --> 00:08:39,445
Sorry for being so logical.

137
00:08:39,445 --> 00:08:43,841
But if I were in her shoes,
it'd be way too hard,

138
00:08:43,841 --> 00:08:46,733
having the husband I love just vanish.

139
00:08:48,449 --> 00:08:52,533
Do you think that someone who lives
according to what they've truly understood

140
00:08:53,409 --> 00:08:55,869
would disappear over and over?

141
00:08:55,869 --> 00:08:57,873
You've got a point.

142
00:08:58,625 --> 00:09:02,141
I think it's totally possible that
she could meet someone better,

143
00:09:02,141 --> 00:09:04,225
fall in love, and get married.

144
00:09:04,225 --> 00:09:06,645
If she ends up happy,
that's all I want for her.

145
00:09:12,893 --> 00:09:16,600
Well, okay. Take care, then.

146
00:09:17,365 --> 00:09:20,281
See you again on some third Friday.

147
00:09:20,281 --> 00:09:22,057
Yeah.

148
00:09:33,173 --> 00:09:35,009
Huh? This is good!

149
00:09:55,197 --> 00:09:57,269
You're a weird one.

150
00:09:57,269 --> 00:09:59,677
You think so?

151
00:09:59,677 --> 00:10:03,649
I don't know. You're the first person
I've met who just disappears.

152
00:10:03,649 --> 00:10:06,537
Even if I explain it,
it doesn't make sense, right?

153
00:10:07,481 --> 00:10:10,733
No, I get the feeling.

154
00:10:12,405 --> 00:10:15,277
Well, it's the kind of work I do, so...

155
00:10:15,277 --> 00:10:17,041
when something bad happens,

156
00:10:17,041 --> 00:10:23,277
I want to just skip my destination
and drive far, far away.

157
00:10:23,277 --> 00:10:25,001
Really?

158
00:10:25,001 --> 00:10:28,353
But I do actually go where I'm supposed to.

159
00:10:31,109 --> 00:10:33,569
Even while wanting to run away,

160
00:10:33,569 --> 00:10:37,949
I bet most people are just
forcing themselves to stay put.

161
00:10:37,949 --> 00:10:40,617
Normal people too? Everyone?

162
00:10:40,617 --> 00:10:44,073
Everyone wants to run away from something.

163
00:10:50,181 --> 00:10:51,831
So...

164
00:10:52,557 --> 00:10:56,313
if I talk about it, people might understand?

165
00:10:57,173 --> 00:10:59,481
If you talk about it? Yeah.

166
00:10:59,481 --> 00:11:00,745
Because if you just disappear,

167
00:11:00,745 --> 00:11:02,533
without saying a word,

168
00:11:02,533 --> 00:11:05,266
that's why people think it makes no sense.

169
00:11:11,845 --> 00:11:15,333
I'll try talking about it someday.

170
00:11:46,269 --> 00:11:52,205
[June 30, 2026]

171
00:11:59,393 --> 00:12:00,477
[Nonoka - Please help me.]

172
00:12:00,477 --> 00:12:01,673
[I'm running away from
my abusive husband and need money.]

173
00:12:01,673 --> 00:12:02,705
[I need 300,000 yen right now.
An acquaintance gave me your contact.]

174
00:12:02,705 --> 00:12:04,457
[Sorry for the sudden message.
I'm currently...]

175
00:12:10,297 --> 00:12:12,125
Sacchan.

176
00:12:13,645 --> 00:12:15,214
Sacchan?

177
00:12:15,214 --> 00:12:16,900
Sacchan?

178
00:12:22,113 --> 00:12:23,900
Sacchan.

179
00:12:25,553 --> 00:12:27,361
Sacchan?

180
00:12:32,257 --> 00:12:36,169
Excuse me, which apartment is Mr. Sonoda's?

181
00:12:36,169 --> 00:12:37,897
No idea.

182
00:12:40,153 --> 00:12:43,609
Oh, I see. Sorry, I haven't heard anything.

183
00:12:43,609 --> 00:12:46,153
<i>If she contacts us,
I'll let you know right away.</i>

184
00:12:46,153 --> 00:12:48,281
Okay, please do.

185
00:12:48,281 --> 00:12:51,761
Well, if anything comes up...

186
00:12:51,761 --> 00:12:53,529
Excuse me.

187
00:12:55,917 --> 00:12:58,300
Seriously?

188
00:13:11,665 --> 00:13:15,641
[Café Hikouki]

189
00:13:15,641 --> 00:13:17,465
Huh?

190
00:13:19,029 --> 00:13:21,497
She hasn't been here.

191
00:13:21,497 --> 00:13:23,109
No messages either?

192
00:13:23,109 --> 00:13:25,057
None.

193
00:13:25,057 --> 00:13:27,101
Did she call here?

194
00:13:27,101 --> 00:13:29,333
There's no way she would.

195
00:13:30,049 --> 00:13:31,785
I see.

196
00:13:31,785 --> 00:13:33,365
What about her stuff?

197
00:13:33,365 --> 00:13:37,373
She only took her phone.
Her wallet and purse are still at home.

198
00:13:37,373 --> 00:13:40,017
You think there's any chance she came here?

199
00:13:40,017 --> 00:13:42,885
I think she went to
Mrs. Sonoda's husband's place.

200
00:13:42,885 --> 00:13:46,185
What? You really think so?

201
00:13:46,185 --> 00:13:48,501
- Do you have his number?
- No, I don't.

202
00:13:48,501 --> 00:13:50,241
I don't really like him.

203
00:13:50,241 --> 00:13:52,957
Doesn't he just seem subtly off-putting?

204
00:13:52,957 --> 00:13:56,269
Sacchan left without taking a jacket.

205
00:13:56,269 --> 00:13:59,453
She hates air conditioning, so I'm worried.

206
00:13:59,453 --> 00:14:03,989
If she gets cold, she can just buy something
at Uniqlo using PayPay.

207
00:14:16,177 --> 00:14:18,353
[Shimakuro One-Piece Dress]

208
00:14:45,585 --> 00:14:47,937
Hello, this is Café Hikouki.

209
00:14:49,361 --> 00:14:50,525
Huh?

210
00:14:50,525 --> 00:14:53,254
[Jojodo - Buyer of Used Books]

211
00:14:55,117 --> 00:14:57,381
One moment, please.

212
00:14:58,305 --> 00:15:01,661
Oh, wait. Are you that...
disappearing husband?

213
00:15:01,661 --> 00:15:05,237
Yes, nice to meet you. I'm Mitsuru Seo.

214
00:15:05,237 --> 00:15:08,329
Did you just say yes?
I can't believe you said that.

215
00:15:08,329 --> 00:15:10,965
Has my wife been here?

216
00:15:12,029 --> 00:15:14,205
No, she hasn't.

217
00:15:15,245 --> 00:15:21,481
Wait, so... did she disappear on you
to get back at you for disappearing?

218
00:15:21,481 --> 00:15:24,421
Sorry, my apologies.

219
00:15:24,421 --> 00:15:26,309
Hey, wait.

220
00:15:34,189 --> 00:15:36,293
Oh, sorry. Sorry.

221
00:15:36,293 --> 00:15:39,377
I got a little interrupted.

222
00:15:40,021 --> 00:15:41,905
Hey, Mr. Satake.

223
00:15:42,873 --> 00:15:45,309
Do you have someone you can talk to
about stuff that you can't tell your wife?

224
00:15:45,309 --> 00:15:47,917
- Like a friend from school?
- Right.

225
00:15:47,917 --> 00:15:49,613
Maybe my ex-girlfriend.

226
00:15:49,613 --> 00:15:52,265
- Huh?
- There's nothing between us now,

227
00:15:52,265 --> 00:15:54,877
but we still get along,
so we grab drinks once in a while.

228
00:15:54,877 --> 00:15:56,093
It's just easy.

229
00:15:56,093 --> 00:15:58,005
Does your wife know?

230
00:15:58,005 --> 00:15:59,925
Well, I just tell her that
I'm out with a friend.

231
00:15:59,925 --> 00:16:01,937
And I always let her know before I go.

232
00:16:03,661 --> 00:16:05,333
Everyone reacts that exact same way.

233
00:16:05,333 --> 00:16:07,600
Because from your wife's perspective...

234
00:16:08,849 --> 00:16:10,401
You should at least be honest about it.

235
00:16:10,401 --> 00:16:13,897
No, no, no. She wouldn't believe me anyway,
and fighting about it is a pain.

236
00:16:13,897 --> 00:16:17,717
But it's a good outlet for me,
so I don't want to cut ties either.

237
00:16:25,489 --> 00:16:29,217
[Did your talk go okay?]

238
00:16:37,189 --> 00:16:39,077
Huh?

239
00:16:49,857 --> 00:16:53,081
It's Sakiko. Sorry, I got lost.

240
00:16:53,081 --> 00:16:54,801
Where am I?

241
00:16:58,765 --> 00:17:00,905
Oh, this way?

242
00:17:00,905 --> 00:17:02,766
Toward the mountains, right?

243
00:17:04,161 --> 00:17:05,781
Let's get curry, then.

244
00:17:05,781 --> 00:17:07,557
I'm fine with one that's not mild.

245
00:17:07,557 --> 00:17:10,593
No way. You couldn't handle
the spicy one last time.

246
00:17:10,593 --> 00:17:12,085
- I'm fine now.
- No, you're not.

247
00:17:12,085 --> 00:17:14,366
- Really, I'm okay.
- Good evening.

248
00:17:15,173 --> 00:17:18,325
- Good evening.
- Has Sacchan been by your place?

249
00:17:20,577 --> 00:17:23,981
- Excuse me?
- She left last night and didn't come back.

250
00:17:23,981 --> 00:17:26,485
- What?
- Is he a friend of Sacchan's?

251
00:17:28,217 --> 00:17:29,985
Yeah.

252
00:17:32,037 --> 00:17:35,377
Please don't say anything unnecessary
in front of the kid.

253
00:17:35,993 --> 00:17:37,501
Come in.

254
00:17:37,501 --> 00:17:40,993
- I'm home.
- I'm home.

255
00:17:45,553 --> 00:17:49,417
Sho, you can go watch videos
in the other room.

256
00:17:49,417 --> 00:17:51,505
For how long?

257
00:17:51,505 --> 00:17:53,225
Until dinner's ready.

258
00:17:53,225 --> 00:17:54,665
The whole time?

259
00:17:54,665 --> 00:17:56,497
You can take your time cooking.

260
00:17:56,497 --> 00:17:58,189
Okay, got it.

261
00:17:58,189 --> 00:18:00,093
Do you want to watch with me, mister?

262
00:18:00,093 --> 00:18:02,917
No, he won't be watching with you.

263
00:18:02,917 --> 00:18:04,877
Go ahead, I'll call you when dinner's ready.

264
00:18:04,877 --> 00:18:06,600
Okay.

265
00:18:12,985 --> 00:18:14,693
He looks like her mother.

266
00:18:15,453 --> 00:18:18,541
He's gotten over his shyness,
so he seems even more like her now.

267
00:18:18,541 --> 00:18:20,821
And he talks a lot.

268
00:18:22,953 --> 00:18:25,201
Have you been in touch with her?

269
00:18:25,201 --> 00:18:29,449
I sent her a message on LINE last night,
but she hasn't read it.

270
00:18:29,449 --> 00:18:31,413
I was worried.

271
00:18:31,413 --> 00:18:34,349
- Maybe we should go to the police.
- That won't do any good.

272
00:18:34,349 --> 00:18:38,385
A wife leaving home after a fight
isn't a criminal case.

273
00:18:38,385 --> 00:18:41,097
So they won't search for her.

274
00:18:41,097 --> 00:18:44,041
You might want to file
a missing person report,

275
00:18:44,041 --> 00:18:46,805
but in this case,
don't expect much from the police.

276
00:18:46,805 --> 00:18:48,617
You seem to know a lot about this.

277
00:18:49,561 --> 00:18:55,600
A year ago, I had something similar happen
with someone close to me.

278
00:19:02,993 --> 00:19:06,013
Do you have any idea where she might be?

279
00:19:06,885 --> 00:19:09,365
We've only known each other for a year.

280
00:19:09,365 --> 00:19:12,565
We're just friends who grab a meal
together every now and then.

281
00:19:12,565 --> 00:19:15,789
I don't really know Mrs. Seo that well.

282
00:19:15,789 --> 00:19:20,137
But sometimes it's easier to
talk to someone at that distance.

283
00:19:22,085 --> 00:19:23,861
What is it?

284
00:19:24,481 --> 00:19:26,366
Is this about Azusa?

285
00:19:27,941 --> 00:19:30,001
I didn't mean it like that.

286
00:19:30,001 --> 00:19:32,129
The day of the accident,

287
00:19:32,129 --> 00:19:34,801
she suddenly said that she was going along.

288
00:19:34,801 --> 00:19:37,066
It was Azusa who suggested it, right?

289
00:19:40,925 --> 00:19:44,366
See? I knew it.

290
00:19:46,873 --> 00:19:49,083
So you're making it your fault,

291
00:19:49,809 --> 00:19:52,100
so that I'll hate you? Is that it?

292
00:19:54,229 --> 00:19:56,569
Without something to direct my anger at,

293
00:19:56,569 --> 00:19:59,313
you thought I'd be struggling
after losing my wife.

294
00:19:59,313 --> 00:20:02,673
Excuse me? Who do you think you are?

295
00:20:03,885 --> 00:20:06,497
You're protecting Azusa
out of a sense of superiority, aren't you?

296
00:20:08,897 --> 00:20:11,200
If I'd told the truth from the start,

297
00:20:12,265 --> 00:20:13,705
would you have believed me?

298
00:20:13,705 --> 00:20:15,785
No, I probably wouldn't have.

299
00:20:16,685 --> 00:20:20,749
I'm sorry that
I wrongly suspected you, but...

300
00:20:20,749 --> 00:20:22,817
that's not the issue.

301
00:20:23,449 --> 00:20:25,437
Whether that was the issue or not,

302
00:20:25,437 --> 00:20:27,965
I became stubborn
trying to make things clear,

303
00:20:28,789 --> 00:20:32,577
to distract myself from
the fact that it's not the issue,

304
00:20:32,577 --> 00:20:35,977
to distract myself...

305
00:20:37,625 --> 00:20:39,981
Anyway, whatever.

306
00:20:39,981 --> 00:20:42,100
It's not like it'll bring her back.

307
00:20:45,381 --> 00:20:49,533
May I pay my respects?

308
00:20:50,613 --> 00:20:52,366
Go ahead.

309
00:21:18,545 --> 00:21:22,473
Sho puts things here, saying that
he'll give anything to his mom.

310
00:21:22,473 --> 00:21:24,800
It's been messy for a while.

311
00:21:32,801 --> 00:21:36,205
I've thought, "I wish you had died instead."

312
00:21:37,065 --> 00:21:38,961
Why did it have to be Azusa?

313
00:21:44,885 --> 00:21:46,777
But...

314
00:21:47,521 --> 00:21:50,345
I guess it's better than
never knowing the truth.

315
00:21:50,345 --> 00:21:53,266
I don't want Mrs. Seo to
have to go through the same thing.

316
00:21:55,605 --> 00:21:58,066
If it had to be one of you,

317
00:21:59,221 --> 00:22:01,277
I wish you had been the one to die instead.

318
00:22:02,069 --> 00:22:04,266
I'll keep thinking that
for the rest of my life.

319
00:22:17,461 --> 00:22:19,533
I'm really sorry.

320
00:22:31,897 --> 00:22:33,817
Sorry for disturbing you.

321
00:22:34,857 --> 00:22:38,300
Can you leave me your contact info?

322
00:22:39,789 --> 00:22:44,553
If I find out anything,
I'll need to let you know.

323
00:22:44,553 --> 00:22:46,269
Sure.

324
00:22:50,145 --> 00:22:53,317
The things we talked about at the laundromat,

325
00:22:54,989 --> 00:22:57,497
like what a birthday gift should be,

326
00:22:57,497 --> 00:23:00,100
or complaints about mom groups
and her mother-in-law...

327
00:23:00,785 --> 00:23:05,189
She said she felt bad always making
her husband listen to her complaints,

328
00:23:07,753 --> 00:23:10,633
- Can I add you on LINE?
- Yes.

329
00:23:12,645 --> 00:23:17,421
So no matter the topic, she'd always end up
gushing about her husband.

330
00:23:17,421 --> 00:23:20,100
I thought that was a real talent.

331
00:23:22,213 --> 00:23:25,400
- I'll just send a random sticker.
- Okay.

332
00:23:27,641 --> 00:23:30,941
[Today - Unread messages below]

333
00:23:34,585 --> 00:23:39,233
Mrs. Seo was kind of the same way.

334
00:23:41,409 --> 00:23:46,533
I think she's just being stubborn,
but she's also lonely.

335
00:23:48,041 --> 00:23:50,066
She'll be back soon.

336
00:24:06,589 --> 00:24:11,117
<i>♪ Let's go eat some crab ♪</i>

337
00:24:11,117 --> 00:24:14,989
<i>♪ Let's go eat some crab ♪</i>

338
00:24:14,989 --> 00:24:19,193
<i>♪ It's just too picturesque ♪</i>

339
00:24:19,193 --> 00:24:24,169
<i>♪ A charming, long, happy beach ♪</i>

340
00:24:24,169 --> 00:24:27,600
<i>♪ La la la la la la ♪</i>

341
00:24:28,373 --> 00:24:30,117
<i>♪ La la ♪</i>

342
00:24:37,069 --> 00:24:39,549
<i>Will that man come visit us again?</i>

343
00:24:39,549 --> 00:24:41,353
I wonder.

344
00:24:42,097 --> 00:24:44,400
He's your friend, right, Dad?

345
00:24:45,573 --> 00:24:47,566
Your mom's friend.

346
00:24:53,629 --> 00:24:55,929
[Where are you?]

347
00:24:55,929 --> 00:24:58,929
[Read]

348
00:25:04,037 --> 00:25:05,537
[Large laundromat]

349
00:25:05,537 --> 00:25:07,261
<i>Now starting the cycle.</i>

350
00:25:07,261 --> 00:25:10,900
<i>Please check for any forgotten laundry.</i>

351
00:25:23,765 --> 00:25:25,745
Good evening.

352
00:25:25,745 --> 00:25:27,277
Good evening.

353
00:25:27,277 --> 00:25:31,129
This heat every day
is just exhausting, isn't it?

354
00:25:31,129 --> 00:25:33,305
It really is.

355
00:25:33,305 --> 00:25:34,965
Are you traveling?

356
00:25:34,965 --> 00:25:36,945
I'm visiting a friend's place.

357
00:25:39,708 --> 00:25:44,141
[Golgo 13]

358
00:25:52,177 --> 00:25:54,500
Good evening. Delivery for you.

359
00:25:56,593 --> 00:25:58,333
Thank you.

360
00:26:00,129 --> 00:26:03,625
[Recipient: Sakiko Seo]

361
00:26:11,237 --> 00:26:16,733
[Your usual supplements arrived.]

362
00:26:33,097 --> 00:26:38,329
[Sacchan's Mom]

363
00:26:39,497 --> 00:26:43,073
<i>Oh, is that you, Mitsuru?</i>

364
00:26:43,073 --> 00:26:45,977
Yes, it's been a while.

365
00:26:45,977 --> 00:26:49,166
<i>What's your deal? Where have you been?</i>

366
00:26:49,166 --> 00:26:50,549
<i>It was another woman, wasn't it?</i>

367
00:26:50,549 --> 00:26:53,837
No, it wasn't. I'm sorry for worrying you.

368
00:26:53,837 --> 00:26:55,777
<i>I'm not worried about you.</i>

369
00:26:55,777 --> 00:26:58,641
<i>How's Sakiko? Is she doing well?</i>

370
00:26:58,641 --> 00:27:00,204
<i>She's so sweet.</i>

371
00:27:00,204 --> 00:27:04,893
<i>I know she's just holding back
on contacting me so that I don't worry.</i>

372
00:27:04,893 --> 00:27:07,677
Yes, she's fine. She's doing well.

373
00:27:07,677 --> 00:27:09,517
- Well, I'll be going...
- <i>Well, that's good if she's fine.</i>

374
00:27:09,517 --> 00:27:12,033
<i>Sakiko's happiness is my happiness.</i>

375
00:27:12,033 --> 00:27:14,197
<i>And since you married her,</i>

376
00:27:14,197 --> 00:27:16,529
<i>you have a duty to make her happy, you know?</i>

377
00:27:16,529 --> 00:27:21,141
<i>Oh, but you know,
just leaving her would be a kindness too.</i>

378
00:27:21,141 --> 00:27:23,245
<i>If it's for Sakiko's sake, then it's fine.</i>

379
00:27:23,245 --> 00:27:27,849
<i>Sakiko could have plenty of
wonderful men besides you,</i>

380
00:27:27,849 --> 00:27:33,709
<i>because she's the gentlest, kindest,
and most thoughtful girl in the world.</i>

381
00:27:33,709 --> 00:27:38,425
<i>In fact, she'd definitely be happier
living here with me.</i>

382
00:27:38,425 --> 00:27:40,817
You're absolutely right. Thank you.

383
00:27:40,817 --> 00:27:42,753
I'll take my leave now.

384
00:28:20,573 --> 00:28:23,365
[Café Hikouki - Temporarily Closed]

385
00:28:25,049 --> 00:28:26,809
Well, I guess it's fine.

386
00:28:26,809 --> 00:28:29,045
I'm really out of practice.

387
00:28:29,045 --> 00:28:31,113
Is it really okay for you to be
just casually making coffee?

388
00:28:31,113 --> 00:28:33,097
Shouldn't you be looking for Sakiko?

389
00:28:33,097 --> 00:28:35,881
I contacted everyone I know,

390
00:28:35,881 --> 00:28:37,929
but I didn't find out anything.

391
00:28:37,929 --> 00:28:39,025
I see.

392
00:28:39,025 --> 00:28:42,129
I've realized that
I didn't understand Sacchan at all.

393
00:28:42,129 --> 00:28:43,161
I really get that now.

394
00:28:43,161 --> 00:28:45,901
Then all we can do is wait.

395
00:28:45,901 --> 00:28:48,589
How long do you think you can wait? A year?

396
00:28:48,589 --> 00:28:53,013
I'll search. First, I need to get more info
from the people close to her.

397
00:28:53,013 --> 00:28:54,625
Info?

398
00:28:54,625 --> 00:28:58,629
It bothers me that everyone talks about Sacchan
like she's a different person.

399
00:28:58,629 --> 00:29:00,633
For example, Sacchan's mom

400
00:29:00,633 --> 00:29:02,992
thinks her daughter is the gentlest, kindest,

401
00:29:02,992 --> 00:29:06,533
and most thoughtful girl in the world.

402
00:29:06,533 --> 00:29:09,829
But Sakiko does seem
positive and cheerful, right?

403
00:29:09,829 --> 00:29:13,513
No, no. Sacchan wouldn't be sad
even if her mom died.

404
00:29:13,513 --> 00:29:15,946
And she'd also give a hearse a thumbs-up,

405
00:29:15,946 --> 00:29:17,633
saying she doesn't want to be at her deathbed.

406
00:29:17,633 --> 00:29:19,861
She's not gentle at all. She's funky.

407
00:29:19,861 --> 00:29:21,873
No way.

408
00:29:23,953 --> 00:29:25,333
Wait, seriously?

409
00:29:25,333 --> 00:29:27,993
And Sacchan isn't positive.

410
00:29:27,993 --> 00:29:31,533
She hates people worrying about her,
so she pretends to be positive.

411
00:29:31,533 --> 00:29:35,745
But basically, she's the type who's
always afraid of failure and loss.

412
00:29:35,745 --> 00:29:38,977
Would someone who hates
being worried about just disappear?

413
00:29:38,977 --> 00:29:43,637
Right. So I don't know
if my image of Sacchan is right either.

414
00:29:43,637 --> 00:29:45,493
I wonder.

415
00:29:46,545 --> 00:29:48,813
Sorry, we're closed today...

416
00:29:48,813 --> 00:29:49,917
Huh?

417
00:29:49,917 --> 00:29:51,765
- Hello.
- Hello.

418
00:29:51,765 --> 00:29:53,729
- Did you ask him to come here?
- Yeah.

419
00:29:53,729 --> 00:29:55,453
What do you mean, "yeah"?

420
00:29:55,453 --> 00:29:57,985
- Come in.
- Thanks.

421
00:30:06,449 --> 00:30:10,313
What's your relationship now?

422
00:30:10,313 --> 00:30:12,741
He's my friend's husband.

423
00:30:12,741 --> 00:30:15,905
He's my wife's friend.

424
00:30:15,905 --> 00:30:17,737
Oh, I see.

425
00:30:18,341 --> 00:30:23,021
From your perspective, Mr. Sonoda,
what kind of person is Sacchan?

426
00:30:23,021 --> 00:30:24,605
What kind of person?

427
00:30:24,605 --> 00:30:28,281
He says he's gathering info
to help him find Sakiko.

428
00:30:28,281 --> 00:30:30,853
Well, I think she's just
a normal, nice person.

429
00:30:34,257 --> 00:30:38,166
Though there are moments
when she's subtly off-putting.

430
00:30:39,693 --> 00:30:42,809
It's like she's carrying a ticking time bomb.

431
00:30:42,809 --> 00:30:44,374
I mean, there are often moments

432
00:30:44,374 --> 00:30:45,921
- when the light in her eyes just...
- Okay, that's enough. Really.

433
00:30:45,921 --> 00:30:48,037
That's enough.

434
00:30:48,037 --> 00:30:50,500
- What a pain.
- Huh?

435
00:30:52,373 --> 00:30:54,389
Excuse me...

436
00:30:55,957 --> 00:30:59,765
I met this person over there,
and he said he was called over here...

437
00:30:59,765 --> 00:31:01,281
Come in.

438
00:31:01,281 --> 00:31:03,049
Who is she?

439
00:31:03,049 --> 00:31:05,221
This is Chizuru, Sacchan's senior from work.

440
00:31:05,221 --> 00:31:06,641
And this is Mr. Miyauchi,

441
00:31:06,641 --> 00:31:10,093
who owns the used bookstore
where Mrs. Sonoda worked part-time.

442
00:31:10,093 --> 00:31:12,037
Do you know Mrs. Sonoda's husband?

443
00:31:12,037 --> 00:31:14,469
Oh, I've heard a lot about him...

444
00:31:14,469 --> 00:31:16,849
- Nice to meet you.
- Hi.

445
00:31:16,849 --> 00:31:18,729
Why have we been gathered here?

446
00:31:18,729 --> 00:31:20,693
Please come here.

447
00:31:20,693 --> 00:31:24,053
I want to hear each of
your personal impressions of Sacchan.

448
00:31:24,053 --> 00:31:28,833
It seems I only knew a tiny part of her,

449
00:31:28,833 --> 00:31:30,477
and I thought that if I could
put together all of your opinions,

450
00:31:30,477 --> 00:31:33,613
I might get a clearer picture
of what she's thinking.

451
00:31:33,613 --> 00:31:34,937
If you've searched that much,

452
00:31:34,937 --> 00:31:36,445
then what's the point
of asking for our impressions now?

453
00:31:36,445 --> 00:31:40,789
I just thought that you all
might still be hiding something.

454
00:31:59,465 --> 00:32:02,985
Is iced tea okay for everyone?

455
00:32:02,985 --> 00:32:05,729
- Yes, thank you.
- Sure.

456
00:32:17,925 --> 00:32:20,857
I've known Sakiko the longest.

457
00:32:20,857 --> 00:32:23,801
Same job, same gender, similar age.

458
00:32:23,801 --> 00:32:27,633
Yes, I do feel like
your data would be the most accurate.

459
00:32:27,633 --> 00:32:29,129
Data?

460
00:32:29,129 --> 00:32:32,565
From your perspective,
what kind of person is Sacchan?

461
00:32:33,317 --> 00:32:37,305
To put it simply, she gets bored easily.

462
00:32:37,305 --> 00:32:40,901
Really? I don't get that impression at all.

463
00:32:43,489 --> 00:32:48,833
She's eaten peanut butter on toast
for breakfast every day since we got married.

464
00:32:48,833 --> 00:32:50,967
She says it's too much trouble
to think about it, and it's good,

465
00:32:50,967 --> 00:32:52,633
so she never gets bored.

466
00:32:53,513 --> 00:32:57,833
Well, maybe it's a bit
different from getting bored.

467
00:32:58,565 --> 00:33:00,666
Maybe it's more like, she likes new things?

468
00:33:02,177 --> 00:33:05,569
But she takes really good care of her things.

469
00:33:05,569 --> 00:33:08,833
And when she buys something new,
she usually talks to me about it.

470
00:33:08,833 --> 00:33:10,137
That's true.

471
00:33:10,137 --> 00:33:12,545
Ever since she met you, yeah, that's true.

472
00:33:12,545 --> 00:33:14,789
Wait, was she different before?

473
00:33:14,789 --> 00:33:17,545
Yeah. Before she met Mitsuru,

474
00:33:17,545 --> 00:33:22,189
she was quick to switch gears,
and she didn't dwell on things.

475
00:33:22,189 --> 00:33:26,265
A lot of people change
when they get married, especially women.

476
00:33:26,265 --> 00:33:30,001
Yeah, especially women.

477
00:33:30,001 --> 00:33:32,933
Has Sakiko always given
the impression of being gentle,

478
00:33:32,933 --> 00:33:34,881
or cheerful, both in the past and now?

479
00:33:34,881 --> 00:33:36,429
She has, right?

480
00:33:36,429 --> 00:33:38,697
Isn't she subtly off-putting?

481
00:33:38,697 --> 00:33:41,285
Did she experience borderline
harassment at work or something?

482
00:33:41,285 --> 00:33:45,157
Everyone has those
superficial impressions of her.

483
00:33:45,157 --> 00:33:47,419
I still only have the impression that
she's gentle,

484
00:33:47,419 --> 00:33:50,493
like this nice older man here.

485
00:33:50,493 --> 00:33:53,685
No way. He's actually the harshest one here.

486
00:33:53,685 --> 00:33:55,165
Oh, really?

487
00:33:55,165 --> 00:33:59,266
But the impression that
he's a gentle person is a fact.

488
00:34:01,113 --> 00:34:05,245
We're not talking about
first impressions, right?

489
00:34:05,245 --> 00:34:08,433
We're trying to figure out
Sakiko's true nature, right?

490
00:34:10,097 --> 00:34:11,914
You only try to see the two extremes,

491
00:34:11,914 --> 00:34:15,413
the convenient side or the harmful side.

492
00:34:15,413 --> 00:34:17,166
Especially men.

493
00:34:19,741 --> 00:34:23,665
Naoto, you only see Sacchan
in a positive light, right?

494
00:34:23,665 --> 00:34:25,025
You never say anything bad about her.

495
00:34:25,025 --> 00:34:26,781
But Sakiko is a good person, isn't she?

496
00:34:26,781 --> 00:34:28,384
- "A good person"?
- That's the easiest,

497
00:34:28,384 --> 00:34:29,989
most superficial first impression.

498
00:34:29,989 --> 00:34:33,337
Someone who's just okay,
you'd call them "a good person", you know?

499
00:34:33,337 --> 00:34:34,537
I get that.

500
00:34:34,537 --> 00:34:37,085
We look away from
people's true nature, don't we?

501
00:34:37,085 --> 00:34:38,753
Don't just suddenly suck up to her.

502
00:34:38,753 --> 00:34:42,401
You've been alone with her, right?
What did you talk about?

503
00:34:45,657 --> 00:34:49,400
Come on, be honest.
It might be a clue that'll help us find her.

504
00:34:52,829 --> 00:34:55,825
You don't have to try and find her.

505
00:34:55,825 --> 00:34:58,077
She'll just wander back home.

506
00:34:58,077 --> 00:34:59,829
Why do you think that?

507
00:35:00,469 --> 00:35:04,417
When I started working here,
you were in the hospital, right, Mr. Seo?

508
00:35:04,417 --> 00:35:06,185
Yeah, for appendicitis.

509
00:35:06,185 --> 00:35:09,585
Back then, Sakiko was sobbing the whole time.

510
00:35:09,585 --> 00:35:13,125
What shall I do if Mitsuru dies?

511
00:35:13,125 --> 00:35:15,909
I finally found him.

512
00:35:15,909 --> 00:35:18,329
I think he'll be fine.

513
00:35:18,329 --> 00:35:19,837
Because of appendicitis?

514
00:35:19,837 --> 00:35:22,265
She's usually super positive,

515
00:35:22,265 --> 00:35:24,097
but that time she was just so down.

516
00:35:24,097 --> 00:35:25,973
Because of appendicitis?

517
00:35:25,973 --> 00:35:28,353
She looked like the most
miserable person in the world.

518
00:35:28,353 --> 00:35:29,493
Though, once Mr. Seo was discharged,

519
00:35:29,493 --> 00:35:31,653
she was back to her old self in no time.

520
00:35:31,653 --> 00:35:33,501
Yeah, that did happen.

521
00:35:33,501 --> 00:35:37,453
She can't live without you, Mr. Seo.

522
00:35:37,453 --> 00:35:39,221
- No way.
- It's true.

523
00:35:39,221 --> 00:35:41,833
So what was this past year, then?

524
00:35:43,601 --> 00:35:46,529
True, she did manage to
live without Mr. Seo then.

525
00:35:46,529 --> 00:35:48,949
And she handled her work just like always.

526
00:35:48,949 --> 00:35:51,713
See? She can live on her own.

527
00:35:51,713 --> 00:35:53,821
She wasn't alone this past year.

528
00:35:53,821 --> 00:35:56,653
Because Sacchan had Mrs. Sonoda's husband.

529
00:35:56,653 --> 00:35:59,357
Can you stop phrasing it in a way
that would cause misunderstandings?

530
00:36:02,013 --> 00:36:04,125
Anyway,

531
00:36:04,125 --> 00:36:08,685
when Sakiko was with me,
she was just crazy about you, Mr. Seo.

532
00:36:08,685 --> 00:36:12,109
I can't imagine that
she'd just stop loving you that easily.

533
00:36:12,109 --> 00:36:14,921
So she'll be back soon.

534
00:36:14,921 --> 00:36:17,409
Let's think positive, everyone.

535
00:36:18,093 --> 00:36:22,049
But running away doesn't mean
that she hates you, right?

536
00:36:22,049 --> 00:36:24,473
Right. Hating someone
and running away are unrelated.

537
00:36:24,473 --> 00:36:27,205
I obviously love Sacchan too.

538
00:36:27,205 --> 00:36:31,957
Oh, shut up. Your weird version
of "obvious" is such a pain.

539
00:36:31,957 --> 00:36:34,533
If you really love her,
then just stay with her, you idiot.

540
00:36:35,249 --> 00:36:36,857
This is a person's true nature.

541
00:36:36,857 --> 00:36:39,581
Oh, no. This guy's been subtly
off-putting from the start.

542
00:36:39,581 --> 00:36:41,917
He wasn't ever hiding his true nature.

543
00:36:41,917 --> 00:36:43,753
The same is true for you too.

544
00:36:49,881 --> 00:36:52,593
Okay, okay, okay.

545
00:36:52,593 --> 00:36:54,089
Is it my turn now?

546
00:36:54,089 --> 00:36:56,473
Oh, sorry. We've been ignoring you
for a while, haven't we?

547
00:36:56,473 --> 00:36:58,185
Yeah.

548
00:37:00,089 --> 00:37:04,885
I've known Sakiko Seo the shortest
out of everyone here,

549
00:37:04,885 --> 00:37:06,413
and our relationship is the shallowest.

550
00:37:06,413 --> 00:37:08,957
But I do have a thought.

551
00:37:08,957 --> 00:37:11,361
- Okay, go ahead.
- Thinking about her,

552
00:37:11,361 --> 00:37:14,669
I don't think she'd disappear to get revenge,

553
00:37:14,669 --> 00:37:16,141
or to make someone worry,

554
00:37:16,141 --> 00:37:20,621
or for any other reason like that.

555
00:37:20,621 --> 00:37:21,981
I agree.

556
00:37:21,981 --> 00:37:23,601
- I agree, I agree.
- Yeah, me too.

557
00:37:23,601 --> 00:37:27,325
Well, but it's people like her
who act on impulse.

558
00:37:27,325 --> 00:37:31,125
Yes, I think it was an impulsive act.

559
00:37:31,125 --> 00:37:34,553
- Yes, right.
- Exactly. So...

560
00:37:35,473 --> 00:37:38,100
she might be dead.

561
00:37:45,945 --> 00:37:49,400
You ran away to the mountains, right?

562
00:37:50,013 --> 00:37:51,639
So maybe your wife...

563
00:37:52,782 --> 00:37:54,400
went to the ocean.

564
00:37:55,573 --> 00:37:58,485
If she has all these different faces,

565
00:37:58,485 --> 00:38:03,017
<i>depending on the person,
the place, or even the time,</i>

566
00:38:03,017 --> 00:38:06,100
<i>she'd change who she was, right?</i>

567
00:38:06,913 --> 00:38:11,817
<i>And on top of that, she gave off
an impression of being gentle and cheerful.</i>

568
00:38:11,817 --> 00:38:16,313
<i>I think she was pushing herself way too hard.</i>

569
00:38:16,313 --> 00:38:19,569
And that would cause her
mental health to break down.

570
00:38:21,341 --> 00:38:28,061
<i>Everything that had accumulated
would just burst out from some tiny trigger.</i>

571
00:38:28,897 --> 00:38:35,466
Like a taut string that just snaps
from some little thing.

572
00:38:40,953 --> 00:38:44,005
- That's so inappropriate. Inappropriate.
- Huh?

573
00:38:44,005 --> 00:38:46,533
It's an unhappy ending,

574
00:38:47,237 --> 00:38:48,917
but there is one good point.

575
00:38:48,917 --> 00:38:50,445
No, there isn't.

576
00:38:50,445 --> 00:38:53,353
You, and you.

577
00:38:53,353 --> 00:38:56,125
You'll be linked by a strong bond.

578
00:38:56,125 --> 00:38:58,051
- Huh?
- By sharing a similar situation

579
00:38:58,051 --> 00:39:00,405
and the same pain,

580
00:39:00,405 --> 00:39:03,533
you can support each other and live on.

581
00:39:08,121 --> 00:39:10,417
What?

582
00:39:10,417 --> 00:39:13,641
No, it's fine. She's alive.

583
00:39:13,641 --> 00:39:17,269
Yeah. Sakiko wouldn't make that choice.
She definitely wouldn't.

584
00:39:17,269 --> 00:39:19,817
Right. And after all,
it's just a possibility.

585
00:39:19,817 --> 00:39:22,593
- It's just that the possibility exists.
- No way.

586
00:39:22,593 --> 00:39:25,053
It's not that there's a possibility...

587
00:39:25,905 --> 00:39:27,789
But it's not impossible either.

588
00:39:29,809 --> 00:39:32,381
The more serious and proper a person is,

589
00:39:33,889 --> 00:39:37,177
the more they're prone to mental illness...

590
00:39:37,177 --> 00:39:42,901
And it's the insensitive ones
who survive shamelessly.

591
00:39:47,517 --> 00:39:49,333
- Well...
- Well...

592
00:39:50,249 --> 00:39:52,885
Oh, sorry. You go ahead.

593
00:39:52,885 --> 00:39:54,561
I'm fine going last.

594
00:39:54,561 --> 00:39:57,145
Oh no, what I want to say is difficult
to talk about. Please, go ahead.

595
00:39:57,145 --> 00:39:58,865
Anyone can go ahead.

596
00:39:58,865 --> 00:40:01,749
- All right, then I'll go.
- You, be quiet.

597
00:40:01,749 --> 00:40:04,693
It's just that... well, actually,

598
00:40:04,693 --> 00:40:09,809
Sakiko Seo has contacted me.

599
00:40:13,305 --> 00:40:15,849
What? You, Mr. Miyauchi?

600
00:40:15,849 --> 00:40:18,953
Yes. She called my shop.

601
00:40:21,785 --> 00:40:23,661
Oh, sorry. Sorry.

602
00:40:23,661 --> 00:40:25,565
You heard that, right?

603
00:40:26,305 --> 00:40:28,021
I do social media,

604
00:40:28,021 --> 00:40:30,933
and she said she saw the number
listed there and called.

605
00:40:30,933 --> 00:40:32,833
Well, I guess it pays to put it out there.

606
00:40:32,833 --> 00:40:35,009
- What did Sacchan... wait, why you...
- That's...

607
00:40:35,009 --> 00:40:37,817
- Well...
- Well, what? What is it?

608
00:40:38,577 --> 00:40:43,681
- What we're about to say...
- It's the same thing.

609
00:40:43,681 --> 00:40:45,253
What?

610
00:40:45,253 --> 00:40:46,743
I called her cell phone,

611
00:40:46,743 --> 00:40:49,233
- and Sakiko answered like normal.
- What?

612
00:40:49,233 --> 00:40:53,369
Sakiko called the coffee shop's phone,
just like you used to do, Mr. Seo.

613
00:40:53,369 --> 00:40:56,705
- Huh?
- I got a reply from Mrs. Seo on LINE.

614
00:40:56,705 --> 00:40:59,441
- Huh?
- She wouldn't tell me where she is,

615
00:40:59,441 --> 00:41:01,685
but she said that she's safe,
and that I shouldn't worry.

616
00:41:01,685 --> 00:41:03,865
She said that she's sorry for worrying us.

617
00:41:03,865 --> 00:41:07,629
And she told me to keep it a secret
from her husband.

618
00:41:07,629 --> 00:41:08,713
Same here.

619
00:41:08,713 --> 00:41:09,881
And same here.

620
00:41:09,881 --> 00:41:13,101
I also heard the same thing from her.

621
00:41:19,649 --> 00:41:22,325
Why did you all keep quiet?

622
00:41:22,325 --> 00:41:26,377
- Because she told us to keep quiet.
- Even so...

623
00:41:26,377 --> 00:41:27,633
I'm sorry.

624
00:41:27,633 --> 00:41:29,993
But even with a panicked,

625
00:41:29,993 --> 00:41:33,666
grieving husband right in front of us,

626
00:41:33,666 --> 00:41:37,357
the real reason we all stayed quiet

627
00:41:40,013 --> 00:41:45,409
was because we thought that Sakiko Seo
had shared a secret only with us,

628
00:41:45,409 --> 00:41:49,749
and we were kind of basking
in that feeling of superiority, right?

629
00:41:49,749 --> 00:41:51,589
Wasn't that it?

630
00:41:53,297 --> 00:41:56,437
When a woman like that asks you for a favor,

631
00:41:56,437 --> 00:41:58,217
it feels good.

632
00:41:58,217 --> 00:42:00,597
But when you find out that
she did the same thing

633
00:42:00,597 --> 00:42:02,300
with several other people as well,

634
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:05,233
well, you feel a little played.

635
00:42:06,617 --> 00:42:09,500
This is getting interesting.

636
00:42:10,529 --> 00:42:13,941
A Sakiko Seo whom we don't know

637
00:42:14,861 --> 00:42:17,889
might be out there somewhere.

638
00:42:40,765 --> 00:42:44,281
- Hey!
- Hey!

639
00:42:47,309 --> 00:42:49,165
Hey!

640
00:42:57,845 --> 00:43:00,133
Thanks.

641
00:43:00,133 --> 00:43:01,589
- You found that one?
- That's nice!

642
00:43:01,589 --> 00:43:03,449
Did you find it over there?

643
00:43:03,449 --> 00:43:05,333
Sakiko!

644
00:43:11,489 --> 00:43:12,949
He's holding a fish!

645
00:43:12,949 --> 00:43:14,900
Let's go, let's go, let's go.

646
00:43:15,709 --> 00:43:17,477
Let's go.
